lled cones help eyes detect colour. Typical human colour vision uses three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths. These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue. The visual system compares the signals from the cone types; a cone is not simply a tiny pot of coloured paint. A system using three cone types is called trichromatic. A system using four is called tetrachromatic. Hummingbirds have four cone types. Experiments have shown that they can distinguish colour combinations that humans cannot normally tell apart. In this text, ''tetra'' refers to four types of colour-sensitive cone, not four colours in the whole world.","stem":"The three human cone types are sensitive to different ranges of what?","evidenceQuote":"three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths","questionMode":"explicit_retrieval","factualSources":["https://www.nei.nih.gov/research-and-training/research-news/nei-scientists-use-retinal-connectomics-study-color-vision-circuitry-relevant-mood-myopia","https://www.princeton.edu/news/2020/06/15/wild-hummingbirds-see-broad-range-colors-humans-can-only-imagine","https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1919377117"]}},{"externalId":"english-reading-context-repair-20261026-08","sourceQuestionId":"35e1a648-a9ca-4385-8185-9a55bbc859f7","sourceQuestionHash":"93602571e2a1093662af376baa6aa09f7cf4556fb44405694610a47b13055e44","topic":"Reading: locating specific information","questionText":"Read the passage.\n\nMore Than Three Colours\nLight-sensitive cells called cones help eyes detect colour. Typical human colour vision uses three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths. These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue. The visual system compares the signals from the cone types; a cone is not simply a tiny pot of coloured paint. A system using three cone types is called trichromatic. A system using four is called tetrachromatic. Hummingbirds have four cone types. Experiments have shown that they can distinguish colour combinations that humans cannot normally tell apart. In this text, ''tetra'' refers to four types of colour-sensitive cone, not four colours in the whole world.\n\nWhich colours are commonly associated with the three human cone ranges in the passage?","questionType":"multiple_choice","options":["Black, white and grey","Orange, purple and brown","Yellow, black and white","Red, green and blue"],"correctAnswer":"Red, green and blue","explanation":"The supplied passage supports this answer: These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue.","difficulty":"easy","points":1,"timeLimit":180,"language":"en","eligibleGrades":[7,8],"hints":[],"tags":["reading","self-contained passage","editorial repair candidate"],"taxonomies":[{"primarySkillCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information","atomicSubskillCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information.locate-detail","evidenceFocusCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information.locate-detail.focus.esl-short-text-detail","assessmentProcessCode":"BH-AO1","cognitiveProcess":"understand","evidenceStatement":"Retrieves an explicitly stated detail from the self-contained passage."}],"readingContext":{"passageId":"colour","passageText":"Light-sensitive cells called cones help eyes detect colour. Typical human colour vision uses three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths. These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue. The visual system compares the signals from the cone types; a cone is not simply a tiny pot of coloured paint. A system using three cone types is called trichromatic. A system using four is called tetrachromatic. Hummingbirds have four cone types. Experiments have shown that they can distinguish colour combinations that humans cannot normally tell apart. In this text, ''tetra'' refers to four types of colour-sensitive cone, not four colours in the whole world.","stem":"Which colours are commonly associated with the three human cone ranges in the passage?","evidenceQuote":"These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue.","questionMode":"explicit_retrieval","factualSources":["https://www.nei.nih.gov/research-and-training/research-news/nei-scientists-use-retinal-connectomics-study-color-vision-circuitry-relevant-mood-myopia","https://www.princeton.edu/news/2020/06/15/wild-hummingbirds-see-broad-range-colors-humans-can-only-imagine","https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1919377117"]}},{"externalId":"english-reading-context-repair-20261026-09","sourceQuestionId":"78f32906-bec1-42ec-8acf-4dafaf6d1ba4","sourceQuestionHash":"d8f8ac25de3f096fc5d2d96463ca68e621ee0ae4e7c121575472cd29082cecce","topic":"Reading: locating specific information","questionText":"Read the passage.\n\nMore Than Three Colours\nLight-sensitive cells called cones help eyes detect colour. Typical human colour vision uses three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths. These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue. The visual system compares the signals from the cone types; a cone is not simply a tiny pot of coloured paint. A system using three cone types is called trichromatic. A system using four is called tetrachromatic. Hummingbirds have four cone types. Experiments have shown that they can distinguish colour combinations that humans cannot normally tell apart. In this text, ''tetra'' refers to four types of colour-sensitive cone, not four colours in the whole world.\n\nWhat does tetrachromatic mean in this passage?","questionType":"multiple_choice","options":["Using four types of colour-sensitive cone","Seeing exactly four colours","Having no cone cells","Using only red and green signals"],"correctAnswer":"Using four types of colour-sensitive cone","explanation":"The supplied passage supports this answer: A system using four is called tetrachromatic.","difficulty":"easy","points":1,"timeLimit":180,"language":"en","eligibleGrades":[8],"hints":[],"tags":["r