e Colours\nLight-sensitive cells called cones help eyes detect colour. Typical human colour vision uses three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths. These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue. The visual system compares the signals from the cone types; a cone is not simply a tiny pot of coloured paint. A system using three cone types is called trichromatic. A system using four is called tetrachromatic. Hummingbirds have four cone types. Experiments have shown that they can distinguish colour combinations that humans cannot normally tell apart. In this text, ''tetra'' refers to four types of colour-sensitive cone, not four colours in the whole world.\n\nWhat does trichromatic mean in this passage?","questionType":"multiple_choice","options":["Seeing only three objects","Using three cone types","Having three eyes","Using four cone types"],"correctAnswer":"Using three cone types","explanation":"The supplied passage supports this answer: A system using three cone types is called trichromatic.","difficulty":"easy","points":1,"timeLimit":180,"language":"en","eligibleGrades":[7,8],"hints":[],"tags":["reading","self-contained passage","editorial repair candidate"],"taxonomies":[{"primarySkillCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information","atomicSubskillCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information.locate-detail","evidenceFocusCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information.locate-detail.focus.esl-short-text-detail","assessmentProcessCode":"BH-AO1","cognitiveProcess":"understand","evidenceStatement":"Retrieves an explicitly stated detail from the self-contained passage."}],"readingContext":{"passageId":"colour","passageText":"Light-sensitive cells called cones help eyes detect colour. Typical human colour vision uses three cone types, which are sensitive to different ranges of light wavelengths. These ranges are commonly associated with red, green and blue. The visual system compares the signals from the cone types; a cone is not simply a tiny pot of coloured paint. A system using three cone types is called trichromatic. A system using four is called tetrachromatic. Hummingbirds have four cone types. Experiments have shown that they can distinguish colour combinations that humans cannot normally tell apart. In this text, ''tetra'' refers to four types of colour-sensitive cone, not four colours in the whole world.","stem":"What does trichromatic mean in this passage?","evidenceQuote":"A system using three cone types is called trichromatic.","questionMode":"explicit_retrieval","factualSources":["https://www.nei.nih.gov/research-and-training/research-news/nei-scientists-use-retinal-connectomics-study-color-vision-circuitry-relevant-mood-myopia","https://www.princeton.edu/news/2020/06/15/wild-hummingbirds-see-broad-range-colors-humans-can-only-imagine","https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1919377117"]}},{"externalId":"english-reading-context-repair-20261026-11","sourceQuestionId":"ffa53133-92dc-4f14-947f-7b4d75301cdf","sourceQuestionHash":"825b62a5672eae5457b4a43b126c081044d54d7c5af65a96f0090fe1e4bfa183","topic":"Reading: locating specific information","questionText":"Read the passage.\n\nReading a Historical Estimate\nA CDC history describes how the influenza pandemic spread worldwide during 1918-1919. It estimates that about 500 million people became infected, roughly one-third of the world''s population at the time. The same account gives an estimate of at least 50 million deaths worldwide. These are historical estimates, rather than an exact count of every person. The passage identifies 1918-1919 as the period of worldwide spread. It does not give an exact percentage of the world''s population who died.\n\nWhich period does the passage identify for the pandemic’s worldwide spread?","questionType":"multiple_choice","options":["1796-1798","1853-1855","1918-1919","1967-1977"],"correctAnswer":"1918-1919","explanation":"The supplied passage supports this answer: the influenza pandemic spread worldwide during 1918-1919","difficulty":"easy","points":1,"timeLimit":180,"language":"en","eligibleGrades":[8],"hints":[],"tags":["reading","self-contained passage","editorial repair candidate"],"taxonomies":[{"primarySkillCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information","atomicSubskillCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information.locate-detail","evidenceFocusCode":"eng.reading.explicit-information.locate-detail.focus.esl-short-text-detail","assessmentProcessCode":"BH-AO1","cognitiveProcess":"understand","evidenceStatement":"Retrieves an explicitly stated detail from the self-contained passage."}],"readingContext":{"passageId":"flu","passageText":"A CDC history describes how the influenza pandemic spread worldwide during 1918-1919. It estimates that about 500 million people became infected, roughly one-third of the world''s population at the time. The same account gives an estimate of at least 50 million deaths worldwide. These are historical estimates, rather than an exact count of every person. The passage identifies 1918-1919 as the period of worldwide spread. It does not give an exact percentage of the world''s population who died.","stem":"Which period does the passage identify for the pandemic’s worldwide spread?","evidenceQuote":"the influenza pandemic spread worldwide during 1918-1919","questionMode":"explicit_retrieval","factualSources":["https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/flu/pandemic-resources/1918-commemoration/1918-pandemic-history.htm"]}},{"externalId":"english-reading-context-repair-20261026-12","sourceQuestionId":"59275387-f365-e73c-4fd8-eb29a9b5d130","sourceQuestionHash":"bb9fcb6e20bc2cb5fc83b95ca3e9e4d4d42db117a067a6b2f109fff5087a1a06","topic":"Reading: evidence for an inference","questionText":"Read the passage.\n\nThe Old Map\nThe guide pointed towards a narrow track and said that it would take us straight to the village. I nodded, but I did not put away my old map. At the first turning, I stopped to compare his directions with the lines on the paper. When he pointed left again, I checked the map a second time before following. The others were already chatting about lunch. I stayed quiet and kept the map open in my hands.\n\nWhich detail best supports the inference that the narrator doubts the guide’s directions?","questionType":"multiple_choice","options":["The guide points towards a narrow track","The others talk about lunch","The narrator is walking towards a village","The narrator repeatedly checks the directions against an old map"],"correctAnswer":"The narrator repeatedly checks the directions against an old map","explanation":"The supplied passage supports this answer: When he pointed left again, I checked the map a second time before following.","difficulty":"medium","points":1,"timeLimit":180,"language":"en","eligibleGrades":[7],"hints":[],"tags":["reading","self-contained passage","editorial repair candidate"],"taxonomies":[{"primarySkillCode":"eng.reading.inference","atomicSubskillCode":"eng.reading.inference.attitude-feeling","evidenceFocusCode":"eng.reading.inference.attitude-feeling.focus.select-evidence-that-establishes-narrator-distrust-130ba7e575","assessmentProcessCode":"BH-AO3","cognitiveProcess":"analyze","evidenceStatement":"Selects an explicitly supplied action as evidence of narrator distrust."}],"readingContext":{"passageId":"guide","passageText":"The guide pointed towards a narrow track and said that it would take us straight to the village. I nodded, but I did not put away my old map. At the first turning, I stopped to compare his directions with the lines on the paper. When he pointed left again, I checked the map a second time before following. The others were already chatting about lunch. I stayed quiet and kept the map open in my hands.","stem":"Which detail best supports the inference that the narrator doubts the guide’s directions?","evidenceQuote":"When he pointed left again, I checked the map a second time before following.","questionMode":"inference","factualSources":[]}}]}'::jsonb,true);
